          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Polymeric sand reaches walkable firmness within a day or two, and that is what misleads people. Full cure — the point at which the joint has finished releasing moisture — takes around 30 days, and a sealer applied before then locks that moisture under a film where it turns into permanent cloudiness.
        </p>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long to Wait: The Numbers
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time After Installation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foot traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface is firm enough to walk on carefully</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vehicle traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 to 48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joints hold under load</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joint has finished releasing moisture</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Safe to seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 days minimum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longer in cool, humid or shaded conditions</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The 30-day figure is the one that matters and the one most often ignored, because by day three the patio looks and feels entirely finished. Polymeric sand continues to cure and release moisture internally well past the point where the surface is hard, and a sealer is a film that stops that moisture escaping.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Why 30 Days and Not Three</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Hard is not the same as cured.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The polymer sets quickly enough to make joints walkable, but the joint retains moisture deep in its structure for weeks. Sealing at day three traps that water beneath the sealer film, where it produces a milky, cloudy haze across the pavers. The sand is fine; the sealer above it has been ruined.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat 30 days as the floor rather than the target, and extend it when conditions have been against you. Cool weather, high humidity, heavy shade, wide joints and deep joints all slow the release of moisture, and in an autumn installation waiting until the following spring is a perfectly reasonable decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-happens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Happens If You Seal Too Early
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cloudy, Milky Haze</h3>

          <p>
            The characteristic failure. Moisture rising out of the curing sand meets the underside of the sealer film and cannot pass through it, so it collects there and scatters light. The result is a whitish, cloudy appearance across the sealed surface — sometimes uniform, more often blotchy and concentrated over the joints. It appears within days of sealing and does not clear on its own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealer Adhesion Failure</h3>

          <p>
            Moisture at the bond line is also a bond breaker. Sealer applied over a surface that is still releasing water may adhere poorly and later peel, flake or lift, particularly over and immediately beside the joints where the moisture is concentrated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weakened Joints</h3>

          <p>
            There is an argument that sealing over incompletely cured sand interferes with the cure itself, since the reaction depends on moisture movement. Joints sealed early can end up weaker than they should have been, and because they are now under a sealer film, repairing them means stripping the sealer first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An Expensive Repair</h3>

          <p>
            This is the real cost of the mistake. Fixing a hazed seal means stripping the sealer off the whole installation with a chemical stripper, cleaning thoroughly, waiting for everything to dry and cure properly, and then resealing. That is substantially more work, more expense and more risk to the paver surface than simply having waited a month.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="longer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. When 30 Days Is Not Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 30-day guidance assumes reasonable drying conditions. Several factors extend it, sometimes considerably.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Cool weather. Curing slows markedly as temperatures fall, and below about 50°F it largely stalls.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>High humidity. Moisture leaves the joint more slowly when the air is already close to saturated.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Deep shade. A patio under trees or on the north side of a house dries far more slowly than one in sun.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Wide or deep joints. More material means more moisture and a longer release period.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Poor drainage. A patio that holds water in low spots stays wet and cannot complete its cure.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A wet month. Repeated rain through the curing period effectively restarts the drying each time.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Practical Test Before Sealing</h3>

          <p>
            Tape a square of clear plastic sheeting tightly over a joint and leave it overnight. Condensation on the underside in the morning means moisture is still coming out of the joint and the installation is not ready. Clear plastic and a dry surface means you are good to proceed. It is the same test used on concrete slabs before coating, and it costs nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the test over a joint in the slowest-drying part of the patio — the shadiest corner, the widest joints, the lowest point — rather than in the middle of a sunny area. The whole installation is only as ready as its wettest section, because you will be sealing all of it at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sequence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Right Sequence From Sand to Sealer
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Install polymeric sand into dry joints</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bone dry joints, correct depth, filled in stages with consolidation and topped up until stable.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear the paver faces before watering</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Broom and then a leaf blower at a shallow angle. Sand left on the surface cures there and becomes permanent haze.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Water in light passes</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Shower setting, several passes, saturating the full joint depth. Watch for milky runoff, which means binder is leaving the joints.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Keep traffic off for 24 to 48 hours</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Foot traffic at 24 hours, vehicles at 24 to 48, longer in cool or humid conditions.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Wait at least 30 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">This is the step people skip. Let the joints finish releasing moisture before any film goes over them.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Test for residual moisture</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Tape clear plastic over a joint in the slowest-drying area overnight. Condensation means wait longer.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clean the pavers thoroughly and let them dry</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Remove dirt and efflorescence, then allow a full dry day. Sealing over a damp surface repeats the same trapped-moisture problem.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Seal in suitable weather</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Check the sealer’s own temperature range and dry-window requirements, which are usually similar to the sand’s.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Do Not Wash the Patio and Seal the Same Day</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cleaning re-wets the joints you just waited a month for.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The pre-sealing clean is necessary, but it puts water straight back into the joints and onto the surface. Allow a full dry day between cleaning and sealing — longer in cool or humid weather — or you will have waited 30 days and then created the identical trapped-moisture problem in an afternoon.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="seasons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Planning the Whole Project Around the Calendar
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 30-day gap has a practical consequence people rarely plan for: sanding and sealing are not one project, they are two projects a month apart, and both need decent weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes late spring and early summer the natural window in most climates. Sand in May, seal in June, and both operations fall comfortably inside warm, settled conditions. Installing sand in late August leaves the sealing job in late September, when cool nights and heavy dew are starting to make a good result less certain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Have Run Out of Season</h3>

          <p>
            There is no penalty for leaving polymeric sand unsealed over winter. Sealer is a protective and cosmetic layer, not a structural requirement, and properly cured polymeric sand is perfectly capable of surviving a winter without it. Sealing in marginal autumn conditions risks the haze, poor adhesion and blotchiness that the wait was meant to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better plan in that situation is to install the sand, let it cure, leave it through the winter, and seal in late spring once conditions are reliably warm and dry. You will also have had a full season to see how the joints have performed and to top up anything that settled before the sealer goes over it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whether You Need to Seal Pavers at All
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the wait is inconvenient, it is worth being clear about what sealing actually buys you — and it is a genuine choice rather than a required step.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Benefit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How Real It Is</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stain resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Substantial; the strongest argument for sealing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colour enhancement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Real, particularly with wet-look products</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Helps stabilise joint sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Modest with standard sealers, significant with joint stabilisers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Slows weed and moss growth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some help, not a substitute for proper joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protects against freeze-thaw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited on a well-drained installation</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Structural necessity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None; pavers do not need sealing to survive</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The honest summary is that sealing is mostly about appearance and stain resistance. A patio that sees food, drink, barbecue grease and vehicle drips benefits meaningfully. A path down the side of a house that sees nothing but rain and foot traffic gains very little, and the money and the month of waiting may be better spent elsewhere.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Maintenance Commitment</h3>

          <p>
            Worth factoring in before you start: sealing is not a one-time decision. Sealed pavers need resealing periodically, and a sealed surface that is allowed to wear unevenly looks worse than one that was never sealed. Choosing to seal is choosing a maintenance cycle, so it is reasonable to decide against it for a low-traffic area you would rather not think about again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Because the Sand Feels Hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand is walkable in a day and fully cured in about 30. Hardness is not the test. The joint keeps releasing moisture internally for weeks, and a sealer film traps it, producing a milky haze across the surface that only a full strip and reseal will fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing the Pavers and Sealing the Same Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pre-sealing clean puts water straight back into the joints you waited a month to dry. Allow a full dry day afterwards, and longer in cool or humid weather. Otherwise a careful 30-day wait is undone in an afternoon and produces exactly the haze it was meant to prevent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing for Dryness in the Sunniest Spot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The installation is only as ready as its wettest section, because you seal all of it at once. Tape your plastic test square over a joint in the shadiest corner, the widest joints or the lowest point, not in the middle of an area that has been in full sun all week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating 30 Days as a Fixed Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It assumes reasonable drying conditions. Cool weather, high humidity, deep shade, wide joints and a rainy month all extend it. Thirty days is a floor, not a target, and the plastic-sheet test is what tells you whether your particular installation is actually ready.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Squeezing the Seal Into a Marginal Autumn Window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There is no penalty for leaving cured polymeric sand unsealed over winter — sealer is protective and cosmetic, not structural. Sealing in cool, damp, heavy-dew conditions risks haze and poor adhesion. Waiting for reliable spring weather gives a better result and costs nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after polymeric sand can you seal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 30 days. Polymeric sand is walkable within 24 hours and takes vehicles at 24 to 48, but it continues releasing moisture internally for weeks. Sealing before it has finished traps that moisture under the film and produces a milky haze across the pavers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you seal pavers too soon after polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Moisture rising from the curing joints cannot pass through the sealer film, so it collects underneath and scatters light, producing a cloudy or blotchy whitish appearance. The sealer may also adhere poorly and later peel. Fixing it means chemically stripping the sealer and starting again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if polymeric sand is fully cured?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tape a square of clear plastic tightly over a joint overnight and check it in the morning. Condensation underneath means moisture is still leaving the joint and it is not ready to seal. Do the test in the shadiest, widest-jointed or lowest part of the patio rather than a sunny spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to seal pavers after polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Sealer is a protective and cosmetic layer rather than a structural requirement, and properly cured polymeric sand performs perfectly well without it. Sealing adds stain resistance and colour enhancement and helps stabilise joints, but leaving a patio unsealed over a winter causes no harm.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal pavers 2 weeks after polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not advisable. Two weeks is well inside the typical moisture-release period, and the risk of trapping moisture under the sealer is high. If you are pressed for time, run the plastic-sheet test — but in most conditions two weeks will still show condensation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does cold weather affect how long I should wait?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, considerably. Curing and moisture release both slow as temperatures fall, and below roughly 50°F the process largely stalls. In cool, humid or heavily shaded conditions extend well past 30 days, and for a late-season installation waiting until the following spring is the sensible choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the patio during the 30 day wait?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Foot traffic is fine after 24 hours and vehicles after 24 to 48. The 30-day period is about the joints finishing their internal moisture release before a sealer film goes over them, not about keeping the patio out of use. Normal use during that month is expected.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does rain during the 30 days affect the wait?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It extends it. Each significant rainfall re-wets the joints and effectively restarts the drying period, so a wet month can push the wait well past 30 days. This is exactly why the plastic-sheet test matters more than counting days on a calendar.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

