        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Wait at least 30 days. Polymeric sand is walkable in 24 hours and carries vehicles at 24 to 48, and that early hardness is exactly what tempts people into sealing three days later. The joint is still releasing moisture internally for weeks afterwards, and a sealer film has nowhere for that moisture to go.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Treat the number as a floor rather than a target. Cool weather, humidity, deep shade, wide joints and a rainy month all push it further out, and the plastic-sheet test — clear sheeting taped over a joint in the slowest-drying corner overnight — is what actually tells you whether your installation is ready.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then protect the wait. Clean the pavers before sealing, but give them a full dry day afterwards, because washing puts water straight back into the joints you spent a month drying. And if the season has turned, leave it unsealed over winter and seal in spring. Cured polymeric sand does not need a sealer to survive, and a rushed autumn seal is how a good installation ends up hazy.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            One last scheduling note: sanding and sealing are two projects a month apart, not one weekend job, and both need decent weather. Sand in late spring and seal in early summer and both operations fall inside settled conditions, which is the easiest way to get a good result from each.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And if the month runs out before the weather holds, leave it. Unsealed polymeric sand comes to no harm over a winter, and a patient spring seal beats a rushed autumn one every time.
          </p>
        </div>

